The theorem says the ratio tends to $1$. The size of $\pi(x)-\operatorname{li}(x)$ is a much deeper question, and it is controlled by the zeros of $\zeta$:

- de la Vallée Poussin (1899) proved $\pi(x)=\operatorname{li}(x)+O\big(x e^{-c\sqrt{\log x}}\big)$ from a zero-free region ([[zero-free-region]]);
- von Koch (1901) showed that the Riemann hypothesis is equivalent to $\pi(x)=\operatorname{li}(x)+O(\sqrt x\log x)$ ([[rh]]);
- Littlewood (1914) proved that $\pi(x)-\operatorname{li}(x)$ changes sign infinitely often, although no sign change has ever been observed numerically.

In 1948 Selberg and Erdős found proofs that avoid complex analysis entirely. These "elementary" proofs are longer and give weaker error terms.

